import{_ as t,o,c as a,a2 as n}from"./chunks/framework.7w-lhQN6.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"How It Works","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"concepts/how-it-works.md","filePath":"concepts/how-it-works.md","lastUpdated":1793550481000}'),s={name:"concepts/how-it-works.md"};function i(r,e,l,d,c,h){return o(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[n('<h1 id="how-it-works" tabindex="-1">How It Works <a class="header-anchor" href="#how-it-works" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;How It Works&quot;">​</a></h1><p>By the end of this page you will understand the three moving parts behind every number MCP Lens shows you: the definition scan that measures cost, the hook-based usage collection that measures calls, and the snapshot storage that keeps both honest over time. For the commands themselves, see <a href="/MCPLens/quick-start">Quick Start</a> and the <a href="/MCPLens/reference/cli">CLI reference</a>.</p><h2 id="the-two-things-being-measured" tabindex="-1">The two things being measured <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-two-things-being-measured" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The two things being measured&quot;">​</a></h2><p>MCP Lens contrasts two independent measurements for every tool: how many tokens its definition costs on every prompt, and how many times it was actually called. Neither measurement depends on the other, and they are collected by two different mechanisms described below.</p><h2 id="definition-scan-what-a-tool-costs" tabindex="-1">Definition scan: what a tool costs <a class="header-anchor" href="#definition-scan-what-a-tool-costs" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Definition scan: what a tool costs&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>mcp-lens scan</code> (or the extension&#39;s onboarding walkthrough) briefly starts each configured MCP server, calls the standard <code>tools/list</code> method on it, and counts the tokens of the returned definitions. That is the entire operation.</p><div class="language-bash vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">bash</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">mcp-lens</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> scan</span></span></code></pre></div><p><code>scan</code> prints one line per server, <code>&lt;server&gt;</code> the server name, the first <code>&lt;N&gt;</code> the number of tools it exposes, and the second <code>&lt;N&gt;</code> the total definition tokens across those tools:</p><div class="language- vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>- &lt;server&gt;: &lt;N&gt; tools, &lt;N&gt; tokens</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Three properties make this scan safe to run against a live configuration:</p><ul><li>It spends no LLM tokens. Counting definition tokens is local tokenization, not a model call.</li><li>It never edits a client config file. It only reads the configured servers and queries them.</li><li>For stdio servers it starts the process, asks for the tool list, and lets it exit; it does not keep the server running or send it any other request.</li></ul><p>The result, definition tokens per tool, is stored as a snapshot at scan time. It is not a live reference back to the server, so a later change to a server&#39;s tools does not retroactively change a past scan&#39;s numbers; running <code>scan</code> again captures a new snapshot.</p><h2 id="usage-collection-what-a-tool-actually-does" tabindex="-1">Usage collection: what a tool actually does <a class="header-anchor" href="#usage-collection-what-a-tool-actually-does" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Usage collection: what a tool actually does&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Definition cost alone only tells you what a tool would cost if kept. To know whether it is actually used, MCP Lens needs call records from inside the client, which is what the per-client hooks and plugins are for (see <a href="/MCPLens/guide/claude-code">Claude Code</a>, <a href="/MCPLens/guide/codex">Codex</a>, <a href="/MCPLens/guide/gemini-cli">Gemini CLI</a>).</p><p>Each hook writes a small metadata record atomically into <code>~/.mcplens/inbox</code> whenever a tool call happens. Draining that queue into the database is a separate step, and only one component performs it.</p><div class="warning custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">The VS Code extension is the only component that drains the inbox</p><p>This is a current limitation, not a detail. The <code>mcp-lens</code> CLI has no drain path at all. If the extension is never running, the records the hooks write to <code>~/.mcplens/inbox</code> stay in the queue and never reach <code>~/.mcplens/lens.db</code>, no matter how many CLI commands you run. <code>mcp-lens hooks status</code> makes this visible: its <code>pending</code> count keeps growing instead of returning to 0, and <code>mcp-lens hooks doctor</code> raises a warning for the same events.</p><p>So installing hooks alone does not make the <code>calls</code> column fill in on a CLI-only machine. What <code>mcp-lens report</code> can still recover comes from a different path: it backfills usage by parsing each client&#39;s own session logs. For anything those logs do not cover, <code>calls</code> stays at 0 until the extension runs and drains the queue. Nothing is lost in the meantime, since the queued records are drained whenever the extension does run.</p></div><p>Three properties keep this collection path from ever getting in the way of your actual coding session:</p><ul><li>Normal stdout is always <code>{}</code>. The hook never returns data that could alter the client&#39;s behavior.</li><li>It never blocks the client. A slow or failing write does not delay the tool call it is observing.</li><li>It writes no network traffic and opens no runtime; it only touches the local filesystem.</li></ul><p>The record itself is intentionally thin: session, turn and call IDs, the client name, the server and tool name, the observation time, call duration, and whether the call errored. It does not include the call&#39;s actual input or output.</p><h2 id="what-is-collected-and-what-never-is" tabindex="-1">What is collected, and what never is <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-is-collected-and-what-never-is" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What is collected, and what never is&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Collected</th><th>Not collected</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Session, turn and call IDs</td><td>Conversation bodies and prompts</td></tr><tr><td>Client name, server and tool names</td><td>Tool inputs (<code>tool_input</code>)</td></tr><tr><td>Observation time, call duration</td><td>Tool responses (<code>tool_response</code>)</td></tr><tr><td>Whether the call errored</td><td>Assistant response bodies</td></tr><tr><td>Scanned definition token counts</td><td>Source code</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Everything lives locally in <code>~/.mcplens/lens.db</code> (SQLite), with <code>~/.mcplens/inbox</code> as the intermediate queue the hooks write to. The original client logs that some clients also produce are treated as read-only; MCP Lens never modifies or moves them.</p><h2 id="the-one-exception-dependency-analysis" tabindex="-1">The one exception: dependency analysis <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-one-exception-dependency-analysis" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The one exception: dependency analysis&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Every measurement described above runs entirely on your machine. Dependency analysis, the feature that judges whether a tool is safe to turn off, is the single exception: it calls an external LLM using your own Claude or Codex CLI account, and only after you have reviewed and consented to what it sends.</p><p>What gets transmitted:</p><ul><li>Server and tool names</li><li>Tool description text</li><li>Input schemas</li><li>The result of a local static-reference check between tools</li><li>Call order and co-occurrence counts with session content stripped out</li><li>The list of candidates being analyzed and the judgment instructions</li></ul><p>What never gets transmitted:</p><ul><li>User prompts and conversation content</li><li>Source code and file paths</li><li>Tool input values and response bodies</li><li>Auth tokens, environment variables, HTTP headers</li><li>Original session logs and raw hook records</li></ul><p>Nothing runs automatically at first. Right after installation the extension neither scans nor analyzes on its own; the onboarding walkthrough is where you choose the LLM provider, see the expected cost and transmitted data, and consent. Automatic scanning and automatic analysis are opt-in from that point, controlled independently, and withdrawing consent stops the automatic analysis immediately.</p><h2 id="snapshots-not-live-references" tabindex="-1">Snapshots, not live references <a class="header-anchor" href="#snapshots-not-live-references" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Snapshots, not live references&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Every scan and every dependency analysis records the state of the world at the moment it ran: definition token counts, tool bodies, and analysis results are all stored as snapshots tied to a timestamp and a definition hash, not as pointers that get reinterpreted later. This is why re-running <code>scan</code> after a server&#39;s tools change produces a new row instead of silently altering history, and why a report from last week still reflects what was true last week.</p><h2 id="where-to-go-next" tabindex="-1">Where to go next <a class="header-anchor" href="#where-to-go-next" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Where to go next&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><a href="/MCPLens/quick-start">Quick Start</a>: run the scan and see your first cost x usage matrix.</li><li><a href="/MCPLens/guide/reading-reports">Reading Reports</a>: what each column and recommended action means.</li><li><a href="/MCPLens/reference/cli">CLI reference</a>: every command and flag.</li><li><a href="/MCPLens/faq">FAQ</a>: what is collected, in your own client&#39;s words.</li></ul>',33)])])}const m=t(s,[["render",i]]);export{u as __pageData,m as default};
